Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: Copper Knotting
- Story & Context: Deep in Shaft 4, Elias discovered the ventilation system emitted a perfect C-sharp when he dragged his wrench across a loose valve, sparking a sudden, dangerous realization that he could encode his loneliness into the architecture. As the dust settled and the rhythmic clanging echoed through the tunnels, he whispered, "The metal sings, so we don't have to scream," setting the tempo for every shift that followed.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A close-up of a scarred, soot-covered hand sliding a heavy iron wrench down a frosted pipe, sparks scattering like slow-motion embers against a dark, industrial background.

Track 2: "Is the ballast heavy enough?"
- Story & Context: Maren, the senior air-controller, stood before the main intake and confessed to the vibrating steel that she had intentionally clogged the secondary flow to hear the pipe-percussion resonate for five minutes longer. The sound was soft, pillowy, and undeniably treacherous, yet she leaned into the wall and asked, "Is the ballast heavy enough to keep our secrets down here?" while the ambient guitars shimmered like heat haze on a mine-floor.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A silhouette of a woman pressing her ear against a massive, riveted steel wall, with ethereal, golden light leaking through the gaps in the seams of the pipe-joint.

Track 3: The Keel-Haul Hymnal
- Story & Context: Years after the mine was abandoned, a group of urban explorers found the "Keel-Haul Hymnal" etched into the central pipe manifold, a final ode to the brothers who were never recovered. The explorers recorded the pipes as the wind whistled through them, creating a haunting, low-frequency myth that "The pipes drink the wind to keep the mountain dreaming," a line that miners now recite whenever they descend into the deep.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: A haunting, ethereal shot of sunlight filtering through an abandoned, rusting ventilation shaft in the mountains, illuminating a ring of moss-covered iron pipes standing like ancient monoliths.

